{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_onuo5magtm3xnhjmd2fmajttkogpibvinkoe4kpfbqdsfnelxcva","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The project builds cleanly. Summary of what was implemented:\n\n- **`src/tableau.rs`**: `beaufort_tableau` builds a square matrix where `mat[y][x] = alpha[(size + y - x) % size]` (mirroring the C `(j+y) % size` with `j` starting at `size` and decrementing).\n- **`src/encrypt.rs`**: `beaufort_encrypt` finds the column whose top-row character matches the source char, then finds the row in that column where the key char appears, and emits `mat[y][0]`. If no match for the source char, it's passed through; if the key char isn't in that column, the source char is passed through and the key index is rolled back (matching the C behavior).\n- **`src/decrypt.rs`**: `beaufort_decrypt` is the inverse — finds the row whose first column matches `ch`, then the column in that row containing the key char, and emits `mat[0][x]`.\n- **`src/lib.rs`**: \n  - `ssize` returns the byte length up to (but not including) any NUL byte.\n  - `usage`/`help` print the same messages as `main.c`.\n  - `read_stdin` reads one line from stdin.\n  - `main` parses CLI args (`-h`, `-V`, `--encrypt`, `--decrypt`, `--key=`, `--alphabet=`), builds the tableau, then loops reading stdin and printing the encrypted/decrypted output.\n\nVerified by running the C test vectors:\n- `beaufort_tableau(\"abc\")` produces the expected 3x3 matrix.\n- `beaufort_encrypt(\"kinkajous are awesome\", \"monkey\", …)` → `\"26004Fyuv AnK Cs9sqC8\"`, plus the other two test cases, and round-trip decryption restores the originals."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":170,"cache_read_input_tokens":32879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":170,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"pare